<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nur ibn Mujahid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1550–1567</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nur ibn Mujahid was a ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a> from 1550 to 1567, following the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Imam Ahmad</a> ibn Ibrahim. He governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> and built its defensive wall, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugol">Jugol</a>. He could not restore the institutional coherence of Ahmad's state, and Adal was reduced to a regional power conducting annual raids.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nur-ibn-mujahid.jpg" alt="Nur ibn Mujahid" width="277" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A. Davey from Where I Live Now: Pacific Northwest · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Streets_of_Harar,_Ethiopia_(2203217018).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a>, or Nur ibn Mujahid, was the son of Wazir Mujahid ibn 'Ali, one of the cavalry commanders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Imam Ahmad</a> ibn Ibrahim al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a>, and of Ahmad's sister. Accounts of his origin differ. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Harari</a> oral tradition and traditions recorded by Enrico Cerulli make him a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> of the Marehan sub-clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darod">Darod</a>; Mohammed Hassen states that his father was a chief of the Harari people; and the royal chronicle of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galawdewos">Gelawdewos</a> calls him one of the tribe of the Suhawyan, which Solomon Gebreyes Beyene suggests may be an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> or Somali subclan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Ahmad's death in 1543 his widow, Bati del Wambara, led Adal's attempt at revenge, but an invasion of the highlands in 1548 was thrown back. She told Nur, then a commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeila">Zeila</a>, that she would marry him only if he killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galawdewos">Gelawdewos</a>. His first campaign, into the lowlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bale">Bale</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dawaro">Dawaro</a>, failed, but Adal soon recovered, and in 1559 Nur invaded Fatagar. While Gelawdewos's cousin Hamalmal entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> and executed Sultan Barakat ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-din">Umar Din</a>, Nur defeated and killed the emperor at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fatagar">Battle of Fatagar</a>, looted his capital in Wej and displayed his head in Harar for weeks. He then married Bati del Wambara.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a>, migrating north, soon invaded Adal and annihilated Nur's army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hazalo">Battle of Hazalo</a>, with a slaughter that the chronicler Bahrey called unmatched since the Oromo first invaded. Much of the Muslim population fled into walled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>, while the Oromo settled the abandoned Hararghe highlands, and the city suffered a severe famine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to J. Spencer Trimingham, Nur did all he could to help his people recover, but died of the pestilence that spread during the famine. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Harari</a> records of the time describe a devout ruler who forbade un-Islamic conduct among the nobility. Some historians see him as the last witness and protagonist of Islamic greatness in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, and a later Harari poem praises him as the builder of the city. His shrine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> is attributed by the archaeologist Timothy Insoll to the period from the seventeenth through the nineteenth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nur ibn Mujahid” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His nephew and successor <b>Nur ibn Mujahid</b> (r. c. 1551–1567) attempted to rebuild stable government from Harar, including the construction of the city's famous defensive wall, the Jugol, but could not recreate the institutional coherence of Ahmad's jihad state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The city walls, built in the 16th century by Emir <b>Nur ibn Mujahid</b>, enclosed the old town of Jugol.</blockquote><a href="/state/harar-emirate">Harar Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
